# Copy to .env, or let ./install.sh write it for you. Never commit .env.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- executor ---
# The engine drives a headless agent. Pick ONE of the two routes below.
#
# Route 1 (recommended): CLIProxyAPI + a subscription you already pay for.
#   A run spends up to three executor rounds plus an audit pass, so metered
#   billing adds up fast. CLIProxyAPI runs on your machine, signs in with that
#   subscription, and answers in the format the engine speaks, so which provider
#   is behind it is CLIProxyAPI's business. Set ANTHROPIC_MODEL to a model id the
#   proxy exposes. See https://github.com/router-for-me/CLIProxyAPI
#   ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN is the proxy's own local api-key, not a provider key.
ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8317
ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN=
ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-opus-5
#
# Route 2: a plain API key, straight to the Anthropic endpoint, billed per token.
#   Comment out the three lines above and use these instead:
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-opus-5
#
# Only if your proxy serves no Claude models at all (a ChatGPT-only or Gemini-only
#   subscription). The CLI keeps a second, cheaper model for chores that are not
#   the work itself, and with nothing set here it falls back to a Haiku id such a
#   proxy cannot answer. Headless runs ask for ANTHROPIC_MODEL and nothing else,
#   because those chores belong to interactive sessions, so leave this alone
#   unless a run dies naming a model you never chose. Compacting a round that
#   outgrew its context window is the one path that might still reach for it.
# ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_HAIKU_MODEL=gpt-5.4-mini

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- paths ---
# The only tree the worker can reach. Target repos must live under it.
LOOPGRAPH_PROJECTS_DIR=/home/you/projects
# npm cache shared with the container, so gates running `npm ci` in a fresh
# worktree don't re-download the world every round.
LOOPGRAPH_NPM_CACHE=/home/you/.npm
# Your uid, so files the agent writes are owned by you and not by root.
LOOPGRAPH_UID=1000

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- browser ---
# Optional. A run that declares a web app in browser.yaml gets a real Chromium:
# the executor drives it, the engine screenshots the declared pages after each
# round, and the auditor looks at both. It is a separate container behind a
# compose profile, because it is about 1.5GB and most runs never open a page.
# Uncomment to enable, then `docker compose up -d`. `lg start` refuses a run
# that has a browser.yaml while this is off. Comma-separated if you run other
# profiles of your own; install.sh keeps those when it edits this line.
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=browser
# The port Chromium's debugging endpoint listens on, loopback only. Next to the
# dashboard's 8400 and clear of the ports dev servers pick. 8420 unless set.
# LOOPGRAPH_BROWSER_PORT=8420

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- telegram ---
# Required. Runs stop and ask you questions, and the worker refuses to start
# without a way to reach you. `lg approve <workflow-id> A` answers a waiting run
# from a terminal, but it cannot tell you there is one.
# The credentials live in their own file (mode 600), never in here.
LOOPGRAPH_TELEGRAM_ENV=/home/you/.config/loopgraph-telegram.env
LOOPGRAPH_TELEGRAM_BOT=@YourBot
